\vspace{-6pt}
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
\vspace{-4pt}

\looseness=-2
Our conclusions rest on $N{=}2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions plus one VLA-to-VLA case, all causal decoders on a single benchmark (LIBERO); flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} action heads may behave differently.
All findings are correlational ($R^2$ measures decodability, not causal reliance); the $\Delta R^2$--success-rate correlation ($\rho{=}0.583$, $p{=}0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) is underpowered ($n{=}9$); each architecture is analyzed at a single trained checkpoint, precluding characterization of how exit collapse develops during training; and the three-tier gradient has $n{=}1$ at Tier~2.
All evaluations are open-loop---we probe representational geometry on offline demonstrations without closing the loop through execution---so the link between $\Delta R^2$ and task performance remains indirect.
The counterfactual sensitivity metrics differ between action-producing models (Cohen's $d$) and reference VLMs (cosine distance), preventing direct cross-condition comparison; a unified metric framework remains an open problem.
Extending the analysis to additional general-purpose VLAs at Tier~2, non-autoregressive action heads, and closed-loop behavioral validation are priorities for future work.

\vspace{-6pt}
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\vspace{-4pt}

\looseness=-2
$\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ serves as a lightweight screening signal detectable with linear probes alone, without closed-loop rollouts; a VLA whose exit-layer $R^2$ drops well below its mid-layer peak likely under-utilizes its learned representations.
Because action-relevant structure peaks at intermediate layers, downstream modules (reward models, planners) should tap mid-layer features rather than the final hidden state; instruction sensitivity distributed across L1 heads further implies that aggressive early-layer pruning risks degrading instruction following.
Across three architectures spanning distinct backbone families, our analyses consistently decompose VLA instruction routing into two components: training-associated effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) that emerge specifically during VLM-to-VLA transitions, and inherited base-model priors (L1 attention routing) that persist unchanged through fine-tuning.
This cross-architecture consistency suggests these representational phenomena reflect general properties of autoregressive VLA fine-tuning rather than architecture-specific artifacts, providing a reusable diagnostic framework for future VLA development.

\vspace{-8pt}
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
\vspace{-6pt}

\looseness=-2
Our conclusions rest on $N{=}2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions plus one VLA-to-VLA case, all causal decoders on a single benchmark (LIBERO); flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} action heads may behave differently.
All findings are correlational ($R^2$ measures decodability, not causal reliance); the $\Delta R^2$--success-rate correlation ($\rho{=}0.583$, $p{=}0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) is underpowered ($n{=}9$); each architecture is analyzed at a single trained checkpoint, precluding characterization of how exit collapse develops during training; and the observational three-tier gradient has $n{=}1$ at Tier~2, limiting its inferential power.
All evaluations are open-loop---we probe representational geometry on offline demonstrations without closing the loop through execution---so the link between $\Delta R^2$ and task performance remains indirect.
The counterfactual sensitivity metrics differ between action-producing models (Cohen's $d$) and reference VLMs (cosine distance), preventing direct cross-condition comparison.
Extending to additional general-purpose VLAs, non-autoregressive action heads, and closed-loop validation are priorities for future work.

\vspace{-8pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-6pt}

\looseness=-2
We presented a cross-architecture representational analysis of three VLAs spanning distinct backbone families, combining layer-wise action probes, counterfactual perturbations, and attention profiling.
Our analyses consistently decompose instruction routing into training-associated effects---exit collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$) and counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$) emerging during VLM-to-VLA transitions---and inherited base-model priors (L1 attention routing persisting unchanged through fine-tuning).
This cross-architecture consistency suggests these phenomena reflect general properties of autoregressive VLA fine-tuning; $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ provides a lightweight diagnostic, and mid-layer features offer a principled tap point for downstream modules.
